From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 16:46:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: a screen with no outputs still has to look like one
 monitor.

Windows always has at least one monitor, and a lot of code takes that as
given.  MonitorFromWindow and MonitorFromRect with MONITOR_DEFAULTTOPRIMARY
are documented never to fail, and callers size and place their windows from
what comes back.

When the session's outputs go away -- the monitor switched off, a lid closed,
an X server with nothing attached -- there is nothing left to read, so
lock_display_devices failed and every one of those calls returned NULL with an
empty work area behind it.  What that looks like from outside is not a display
problem but a hung program: Word puts its first dialog at the origin with no
size, waits for an answer to a window nobody can see or click, and stays there.
It cost two separate sessions to work out that this is what was happening.

Stand in the same virtual monitor the service window station already uses, kept
to the calling process rather than published over whatever another process may
have managed to read.  Catching up with the window station's serial afterwards
is what keeps it from being re-derived on every call -- 258 times during one
notepad start before, 3 after -- and it has to be read again rather than
reused, because the attempt just made moved it on itself.  A real change moves
it once more, and display_mode_changed() re-reads by force in any case.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 1693fe0088a5a928d774a493a0d65526972f382b)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
index 6ed5b772320..c85eea84e66 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -2916,8 +2916,34 @@ static BOOL lock_display_devices( BOOL force )
 
     if (!ret)
     {
-        ERR( "Failed to read display config.\n" );
-        pthread_mutex_unlock( &display_lock );
+        /* A screen with no outputs at all -- the session's monitor switched
+         * off, a laptop lid closed, an X server with nothing attached -- leaves
+         * nothing to read back.  Windows always has at least one monitor, and a
+         * great deal of code takes that as given: MonitorFromWindow with
+         * MONITOR_DEFAULTTOPRIMARY is documented never to fail, and callers
+         * place and size their windows from what it hands back.  Failing here
+         * instead leaves them computing empty rectangles and putting their
+         * windows nowhere, which from outside is not distinguishable from the
+         * program having hung: Word gets as far as its first dialog, places it
+         * at the origin with no size, and stops there for good.
+         *
+         * Stand in the same virtual monitor the service window station uses.
+         * Publishing it moves the window station's serial on, so this is not
+         * re-derived on every call, and the serial moves again when the real
+         * outputs come back. */
+        ERR( "Failed to read display config, using a virtual monitor.\n" );
+        clear_display_devices();
+        list_add_tail( &monitors, &virtual_monitor.entry );
+        /* Kept to this process rather than published to the window station:
+         * this is a stand-in for something that could not be read, and it has
+         * no business replacing what another process may have managed to read.
+         * Catching up with the serial is what stops this being re-derived on
+         * every single call -- and it has to be read again rather than reused,
+         * because the attempt just made moved it on itself.  A real change
+         * moves it once more, and display_mode_changed() re-reads by force in
+         * any case. */
+        monitor_update_serial = get_monitor_update_serial();
+        ret = TRUE;
     }
     return ret;
 }
